WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:06.573
NARRATOR: Ice melts, cracks, and
pulls away from the mainland.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:13.012
The water is still a
bone-chilling 35 degrees,

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:19.152
cold enough to freeze a
human in a matter of minutes,

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:22.455
but perfect for young polar
bears out with their mom.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:35.535
For most of the year,
their world is ice.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:41.141
Open water is a novelty, which
is why what you're about to see

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:41.908
is so rare.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.615
These two practice a
type of swimming usually

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.183
seen in underwater mammals.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.023
It's called porpoising.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.492
[folk music]

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:51.444
[bears roaring]

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.157
Polar bears live on
an opposite schedule

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:06.593
to other animals in Alaska.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.563
Winter is when they
hunt on the ice and eat.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.099
Summer melts their ice.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.168
They have no choice but to fast.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:23.176
They swim and laze in the
sun with empty bellies.

